{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-25301",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(w)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(w)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{w}^{2}-w-2}{w-5}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(w),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(w) & =\\frac{{w}^{2}-w-2}{w-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w-2)(w+1)}{w-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(w-2)(w+1) & =0 \\\\ w & =-1,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(w)=0$ are $w=-1$ and $w=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
